## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with an extra resource dimension (shields) on a classic grid path. Demonstrates handling of constrained resources, 3D DP states, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP with an additional dimension for resources (k)
- Transition with/without consuming a shield
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridPath(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(NEG)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    for (let j=0; j<m; j++) {
      for (let k=0; k<=K; k++) {
        const v = grid[i][j];
        if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k] + v);
        if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k] + v);
        if (k>0 && v < 0) {
          if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k-1]);
          if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(...dp[n-1][m-1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover one optimal path and its moves?
- How to optimize space to O(n*m*K)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a concrete DP state definition on strings with reconstruction and space-time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: dp[i][t] for substring ending at i with t flips
- Transitions handle 1 vs 0 at i; reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space: O(nk) both; can optimize space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxOnesWithFlips(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const start = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (s[i] === '1') {
        dp[i][t] = (i > 0 ? dp[i-1][t] : 0) + 1;
        start[i][t] = (i > 0 ? start[i-1][t] : i);
      } else if (t > 0) {
        dp[i][t] = (i > 0 ? dp[i-1][t-0 - 1] : 0) + 1;
        start[i][t] = (i > 0 ? start[i-1][t-1] : i);
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = 0;
        start[i][t] = -1;
      }
    }
  }
  // find max and reconstruct
  return {len: 0, l:0, r:0};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact substring without extra storage?
- How would you optimize space further?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: block value defined as distinct counts, forcing explicit precomputation and a clear reconstruction path. Tests DP over prefixes with segment costs that depend on subarray content, not just sums.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix DP with exact block count
- Precompute interval distinct counts C[l][r]
- Reconstruct endpoints via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
// DP sketch for distinct-count blocks
let n = A.length;
let C = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(0));
for (let L=0; L<n; L++) {
  let seen = new Map();
  let d = 0;
  for (let R=L; R<n; R++) {
    if (!seen.has(A[R])) { seen.set(A[R],1); d++; }
    C[L][R] = d;
  }
}
let dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
let par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
dp[0][0] = 0;
for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    for (let p=t-1; p<=i-1; p++) {
      let val = dp[p][t-1] + C[p][i-1];
      if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: exactly k non-overlapping subarrays maximize sum, a knapsack-like DP variant with efficient inner maximization. It mirrors budgeted promotion planning.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: dp[i][t] is the best total for first i elements using t blocks
- Prefix sums P enable O(1) subarray sums
- Optimize inner max with best = max(dp[j-1][t-1]-P[j-1]) up to i
- Reconstruction via pointers to endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(a,k){
  const n=a.length;
  const P=[0];
  for(let v of a) P.push(P[P.length-1]+v);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  dp[0].fill(0);
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    let best=-Infinity;
    for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      dp[i][t]=dp[i-1][t];
      best=Math.max(best, dp[i-1][t-1]-P[i-1]);
      const cand=P[i]+best;
      if(cand>dp[i][t]) dp[i][t]=cand;
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for at-most-k subarrays?
- How would you recover the exact endpoints from the DP table?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a less-traveled DP angle: maximizing a sum of subarray-specific statistics (distinct counts) with exact segmentation. Requires careful state design and backtracking for reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix end i and segment count t
- Subarray distinct counts D(p,i-1)
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Time: O(n^2 k), Space: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDistinctPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  // Precompute distinct counts D[l][r]
  const D = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    const seen = new Set();
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      seen.add(A[r]);
      D[l][r] = seen.size;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const bt = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = D[0][i - 1];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 1; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const cand = dp[p][t - 1] + D[p][i - 1];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; bt[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i = n, t = k;
  const segs = [];
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = bt[i][t];
    segs.push([p, i - 1]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  segs.reverse();
  return { best: dp[n][k], segments: segs };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) memory?
- How would you handle at most k segments instead of exactly k?
- Can you adapt to streaming data or reconstruct segments efficiently for large n?

## Why This Is Asked
Combinatorial DP with partitioning; tests clear recurrence and reconstruction, common in interviews. 

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t] = min over p of dp[p][t-1] + max(A[p..i-1]);
- Track parent to reconstruct partition points; iterate i and t in increasing order.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0,i));
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for(let p=i-1; p>=t-1; p--){
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[p]);
        const cand = dp[p][t-1] + curMax;
        if(cand < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {cost: dp[n][k], par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time using a deque-based technique when max is a sliding property? 
- How would you extend to allow hypothetical negative numbers or to reconstruct explicit subarrays?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over subsequences with a fixed length and pairwise costs, plus explicit reconstruction. It moves beyond contiguous partitions by requiring last-step linkage through prior picks.

## Key Concepts
- Subsequence DP with fixed length
- Min-over-predecessor recurrence
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and practical optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function solve(A, m){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(m+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1]=0;
  for(let t=2;t<=m;t++){
    for(let i=t-1;i<n;i++){
      for(let j=t-2;j<i;j++){
        const c=dp[j][t-1]+Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if(c<dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=c; par[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best=Infinity,bestI=-1;
  for(let i=m-1;i<n;i++){ if(dp[i][m]<best){ best=dp[i][m]; bestI=i; } }
  const path=[];
  let cur=bestI, t=m;
  while(t>=1){
    path.push(cur);
    cur=par[cur][t];
    t--;
  }
  path.reverse();
  return {cost:best, indices:path};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests your ability to structure a dynamic programming solution for exact-k resource selection with non-overlapping intervals. It requires combining weighted interval scheduling with a prefix DP over both task count and index, along with binary search for efficient predecessor lookup and clean reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with exact k constraint
- Prefix DP over tasks (i) and count (t)
- Precomputing p[i] via binary search for O(log n) lookups
- Backtracking through DP table for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function weightedIntervalScheduling(tasks, K) {
  // Sort by end time
  tasks.sort((a, b) => a.end - b.end);
  const n = tasks.length;

  // Precompute p[i] - last non-overlapping task
  const ends = tasks.map(t => t.end);
  const p = new Array(n).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let lo = 0, hi = i - 1, res = -1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
      const mid = Math.floor) / 2((lo + hi);
      if (tasks[mid].end <= tasks[i].start) {
        res = mid;
        lo = mid + 1;
      } else {
        hi = mid - 1;
      }
    }
    p[i] = res;
  }

  // DP table: dp[i][t] = max value using first i tasks for t selections
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(K + 1).fill(0));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= K; t++) {
      // Skip task i-1
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      // Take task i-1
      if (p[i - 1] !== -1) {
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[p[i - 1] + 1][t - 1] + tasks[i - 1].value);
      } else if (t === 1) {
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], tasks[i - 1].value);
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const chosen = [];
  let i = n, t = K;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (dp[i][t] === dp[i - 1][t]) {
      i--;
    } else {
      chosen.push(i - 1);
      const prev = p[i - 1];
      i = prev + 1;
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][K], chosenTasks: chosen.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests classic DP with an additional dimension (at most k intervals) and reconstruction—a common pattern in scheduling optimization problems.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint (at most k intervals)
- Predecessor p(i) computed via binary search on sorted end times
- DP table with an extra dimension tracking the number of selected intervals
- Backtracking reconstruction to output the chosen jobs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueKJobs(jobs, k) {
  const sorted = jobs.map((j, idx) => ({ ...j, idx })).sort((a, b) => a.end - b.end);
  const n = sorted.length;
  const ends = sorted.map(j => j.end);

  const p = (i) => {
    let lo = 0, hi = i - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
      const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
      if (ends[mid] <= sorted[i].start) lo = mid + 1;
      else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return hi;
  };

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const prev = p(i - 1) + 1;
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][t], dp[prev][t - 1] + sorted[i - 1].value);
    }
  }

  const selected = [];
  let t = k;
  for (let i = n; i > 0 && t > 0; i--) {
    if (dp[i][t] !== dp[i - 1][t]) {
      selected.push(sorted[i - 1]);
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], jobs: selected.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: fixed window size with non-overlap, maximizing sum of window maxima.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute windowMax to make transitions O(1).
- Prefix DP with non-overlapping constraint.
- Reconstruction via a take/parent table.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumKWindows(A, n, k, m){
  const windowMax = Array(n+1).fill(-Infinity);
  for(let i=m; i<=n; i++){ // window ending at i
    let mx = -Infinity;
    for(let j=i-m+1; j<=i; j++) mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
    windowMax[i] = mx;
  }
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0; i<=n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let t=1; t<=k; t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if(i>=m && dp[i-m][t-1] + windowMax[i] > dp[i][t]){
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-m][t-1] + windowMax[i];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

